WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.528
Part five.

00:00:01.528 --> 00:00:04.044
Data-driven runs from a CSV file.

00:00:05.167 --> 00:00:09.492
Instead of one customer, let's try three, with different shipping options.

00:00:09.967 --> 00:00:13.879
First, edit the shipping step so it uses ${shipping},

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.964
and let the Assert take the text it expects from ${expect}.

00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:36.317
Here is the data file.

00:00:36.317 --> 00:00:41.309
Its first line names the variables: customer, shipping and expect.

00:00:41.333 --> 00:00:49.000
Each line after it is one run: Bao Tran with standard shipping, Chris Vo with express, and Dana Le with express too.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:56.103
Dana Le's line expects free shipping on purpose, which the shop doesn't offer, so we can see what a failed row looks like.

00:00:57.233 --> 00:01:01.227
In the CSV Run card, choose Buy a book and upload the file.

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:04.046
Pocket QA previews the rows it will run.

00:01:09.033 --> 00:01:14.467
Pick the result format, an HTML report here, and click Start CSV Run.

00:01:14.467 --> 00:01:16.449
Each row is a run of its own.

00:01:16.449 --> 00:01:21.790
The page panel shows which row is playing, and the popup counts passes and failures.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:35.067
All three rows done: two passed, and the third is marked failed, because the page never mentions free shipping.

00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:37.129
Download the result and open it.

00:01:37.129 --> 00:01:43.207
Every row has its data, its verdict, and the step that failed with the reason, ready to share with the team.

00:01:46.833 --> 00:01:51.007
Next, the capture and image tools, for clearer bug reports.
